  "slug": "networks-brain-ba09",
  "summary": "Four preprints apply dynamic connectivity, optimal transport and tensor methods to EEG, MEG and fMRI data from humans.",
  "synthesis": "The four preprints share a focus on how brain activity is organised over time and across regions, rather than on static activation. A dynamic causal model of EEG during breaking continuous flash suppression links top-down prefrontal connectivity to perceptual breakthrough. A Gaussian hidden Markov model combined with optimal transport, applied to resting-state fMRI from 122 multiple sclerosis patients and 97 controls, yields patient subgroups with distinct brain dynamics. Tensor decomposition of OP-MEG data from three epilepsy patients recovers motor beta and visual alpha components, establishing feasibility. Multimodal eye-tracking, pupillometry and EEG during naturalistic learning in 86 participants show that brain-body state dynamics predict subsequent quiz performance. The novelty lies in applying time-resolved, multimodal decomposition to clinical stratification and to learning outcomes.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
